<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rieti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Reate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rieti, ancient Reate, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in a fertile basin of the Velino river. It was a chief town of the Sabines and passed under Roman rule in 290 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-curius-dentatus">Manius Curius Dentatus</a> later drained part of the nearby marshes by cutting a channel that created the Marmore Falls. The scholar Varro and the emperor Vespasian came from its district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rieti.jpg" alt="Rieti" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Antonelli · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rieti_da_Colle_San_Mauro_-_Centro_storico,_2_(mid).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sabine society was rural, made up of villages and small towns such as Cures, <b>Reate</b>, Nursia and Amiternum, with a reputation in Roman eyes for austere virtue.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a></li></ul></section>
